tion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479039 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186478593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479039 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ce5f4636-c6b9-5bae-8b55-2a495ce5b39c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479044 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479044 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479044 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186478586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479044 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dbfbb460-7f02-5f92-88f0-57dd4c8eadf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479061 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479061 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479061 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479061 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $p(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂p}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479061 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10ac6b1a-97da-521c-a5c2-16923318bd3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479061 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $p(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂p}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂p}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂p}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂p}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $n$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂p}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂x} & =2, \\\\ \\frac{∂p}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂\\theta } & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479083 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479083 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479083 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $v(r,\\theta )=r(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ),$ find $\\frac{∂v}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41bc3280-6f3c-5ef7-84dc-f2df8938f974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479083 #edb/ref 1074 "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂v}{∂y}=\\frac{∂v}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $v$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂r}=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }=-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479084 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479084 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479084 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479084 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{3}+{z}^{4},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂r},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43a13109-9c88-5e83-9206-805f97530d84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479084 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +3{r}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479085 